<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medieval philosophy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Medieval philosophy is the philosophy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, from the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> in the 5th century to the 13th and 14th centuries. As independent inquiry it began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in the middle of the 8th century and at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> in the late 8th century. Jewish, Christian and Muslim thinkers sought to recover the learning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and to reconcile sacred doctrine with secular learning, treating the relation of faith to reason, the existence of God, and the problems of universals and individuation. Its golden age in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> West came in the 12th, 13th and 14th centuries with the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and his Arabic commentators.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Two Roman thinkers shaped the early period. Augustine (354–430), strictly a figure of the classical period, was primarily a theologian, but much of his writing is philosophical, and for over a thousand years hardly a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> work of theology or philosophy failed to quote him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boethius">Boethius</a>, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> around 480, became consul in 510 in the kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s Categories and On Interpretation and Porphyry's Isagoge, which brought the problem of universals to the medieval world. Much of Aristotle was otherwise unknown in the West.</p><p>The first significant renewal of learning came when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a> and others, established schools in every abbey of his empire by decree in 787, the schools from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholasticism">Scholasticism</a> takes its name. A lasting revival of learning in the 11th century owed much to Greek thought rediscovered through Arabic translations, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>'s On the soul.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Medieval philosophy is characteristically theological. With the possible exceptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a>, medieval thinkers did not consider themselves philosophers; the philosophers were the ancient writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>. Their work rested on three principles: the use of logic and analysis to find the truth (ratio), respect for the authority of ancient philosophers, above all Aristotle (auctoritas), and the duty to coordinate philosophy with theological teaching (concordia). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>, following Peter Damian, held that philosophy is the handmaiden of theology.</p><p>Faith and reason were heavily debated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a> leaned toward reason, and the Augustinian answer was to believe first and then seek to understand. Other problems were the existence and simplicity of God, the problem of evil, free will against divine foreknowledge, universals and individuation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The high medieval or scholastic period began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anselm-of-canterbury">Anselm of Canterbury</a> (1033–1109), originator of the ontological argument. The early 13th century saw the culmination of the recovery of Greek philosophy, and the translations of William of Moerbeke in the mid-13th century gave a clearer picture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> than the Arabic versions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, founded in 1209, and the Dominicans, founded in 1215, contended for influence in the universities. Bonaventure defended Augustine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertus-magnus">Albertus Magnus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> drew on the new Aristotelian sources.</p><p>From the 14th century the growing use of mathematical reasoning in natural philosophy, seen in William Heytesbury and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-ockham">William of Ockham</a>, prepared the way for early modern science. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanists treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholasticism">Scholasticism</a> disparagingly, as a barbaric middle period, while modern historians see the era as one of philosophical development.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Medieval philosophy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
